## 4. Notes

- **`<file_change>` ignores every fence** (your first rule): `parseFileChangeBlock()` no longer calls `getFence()` at all, so a ``` / `~~~` line can never open, close or mask anything inside an envelope. The block ends *only* when the tag counter (open `+1`, close `−1`, start at 1) returns to `0`; `lastCloseIndex` remains only as a fallback for a body that never balances.
- **Fenced blocks are typed and counted**: `createFenceTracker()` stores the delimiter (` char`) + the type (first info word) and a `lengths` counter. `trackFence()` returns `'close'` only when the counter reaches zero. `parseBlocks`, `isInsideCodeFence`, `codeBlockExtractor` and `DocumentSummary` all use it, so all four agree by construction.
- **One deliberate nuance**: inside a fence block, an *opening* fence of a different language still increments the counter. A closing fence is a bare run and cannot be typed, so if an inner ```mermaid were ignored its closer would close the outer block — that is the "py file with a mermaid docstring" case that must not truncate. `~~~` vs ``` (different delimiter) is genuinely ignored as content.
- No flags left: `inCodeBlock`, `inFileChange`, `nestDepth`, `fenceStack` are gone; `nestDepth`/`inCodeBlock` were replaced by the tracker counter.
- Run `node tests/fileChangeParser.test.mjs` — cases 10–14 pin the reported "ends three lines after a ```" failure plus the counter rules.

